WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.669
Larry Karaszewski: Hi, I’m Larry Karaszewski,
nominated tonight for the THE PEOPLE v. O.J.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:07.270
SIMPSON, American Crime Story with my partner,
Scott Alexander. Film Courage: Larry, obviously

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.610
this is not your first story about [someone’s
public and private life], you’ve done films

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.450
on filmmaker Ed Wood and Larry Flynt, as well.
What attracted you to this story? Larry Karaszewski:

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.539
This story, it’s funny, because it’s the
first time [writing partner] Scott and I’ve

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:23.439
have ever written television and we basically
do movies and we would have never done this

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:29.300
story as a movie, because as a 2-hour package
it would have just been telling people what

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:35.310
they already knew about the O.J. Simpson trial.
Whereas all the offshoots of it, all of the

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:41.630
themes we could discover inside the piece,
whether it’s gender politics or class relations

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:49.660
or birth of the infotainment culture, you
know. And sort of the black problems with

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.060
the Los Angeles Police Department. And so
there was so much we could do with this story

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:59.100
that 10 hours felt like the proper medium
for this story. Film Courage: Were you in

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.680
Los Angeles during the whole [O.J. Simpson]
trial and the Bronco [car] chase? Larry Karaszewski:

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.530
Absolutely. I remember exactly where I was
when the Bronco chase happened. I think we

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.650
all remember where we were during the Bronco
chase. We were writing a script for Disney

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.320
at the time. And it was before cellphones
and all of those things. And as everyone is

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.640
leaving the building, there was a janitor
downstairs with a little black-and-white television

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.670
and it was like “O.J. Simpson is being chased!”
And the entire building all circled around

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:30.830
the janitor’s closet and watched the Bronco
chase [on television] in his closet. Film

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:34.260
Courage: Do you remember the day that the
verdict came out and how the feeling around

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.770
the City [of Los Angeles]? Larry Karaszewski:
Absolutely. Film Courage: So how did this

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:42.600
[writing] opportunity come to you? Larry Karaszewski:
One of the producers on the project, named

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.490
Brad Simpson, was in a used bookstore and
he saw Jeffrey Toobin’s book [The Run of

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.610
His Life - The People v. O. J. Simpson]. And
there was this little idea in Brad’s head

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.729
like “Maybe I should take a look at this?
Maybe it’s time to revisit the O.J. Simpson

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:01.470
trial?” And he read the book and it’s
a terrific book. Jeffrey is also nominated

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.330
tonight. And the great thing about this particular
award show is they give the person who wrote

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.330
the book an award, as well as the person who
adapted it.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:13.692
It was one of those things where we were looking
for a long time what would be the thing [project]

00:02:13.692 --> 00:02:17.590
we would do for television. Scott and I are
usually very deliberate about our decision

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:22.921
about the kind of projects we take. And in
a second we were like “Yes!” in the room

00:02:22.921 --> 00:02:27.569
there. We want to take on Tobin’s book.
Film Courage: So from that time that you met

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:35.110
with [producer Brad Simpson] how much time
has passed since you got the show on the air?

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:40.170
Larry Karaszewski: It was a very long time.
We didn’t treat it like traditional television.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:47.690
We really developed it and researched it and
so it was about three years until it got on

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:52.490
the air and now about a year since it’s
been on the air. Film Courage: When you wrote

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:59.910
the beginning episode, did you have other
people look at it [the script] for a tone

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:04.280
and making sure that whatever your own bias
was or your [writing] partner’s bias was

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.790
not put in to the writing? Larry Karaszewski:
I would say we definitely had other people

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:15.750
take a look at it, but we have our own tone,
so I don’t actually think it was about us

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.790
taking our take away from it. We were never
coming right out and saying whether he did

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.421
or didn’t do it. But there is so much evidence
that sort of point in that direction. We were

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:32.881
just presenting the case in a very unusual,
satiric, comedic tone but the tricky thing

00:03:32.881 --> 00:03:38.271
in this was two innocent people died, so you
never wanted that to be any source of humor.

00:03:38.271 --> 00:03:45.930
But there was an absurdity that was the O.J.
Simpson trial and having The Kardashians be

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.640
a part of it and things like that. So we were
always conscious of trying to keep our tone

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:58.250
and we always had great advice and some co-writers
with us all the time to keep us honest. It

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.040
was very important to us to tell the truth
and do it in an very entertaining way. Film

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.340
Courage: I know all of the legal team became
their own celebrity from it [the trial], Judge

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.910
Ito included. How much research did you do
[beforehand] on let’s say [prosecuting attorney]

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:15.140
Marcia Clark or whomever? Larry Karaszewski:
We did an insane amount of research. I mean

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:18.629
there is Jeffrey Toobin’s book but every
single person involved in the trial wrote

00:04:18.629 --> 00:04:24.559
at least one, maybe two books, sometimes three
books. There is thousands and thousands of

00:04:24.559 --> 00:04:29.909
court transcripts. There are hours of court
TV so it was really just the most intense

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:37.779
research project we ever had. Even the jurors
wrote books. Film Courage: So when you write

00:04:37.779 --> 00:04:42.979
a screenplay do you think that it’s finished
after two drafts? How critical are you of

00:04:42.979 --> 00:04:45.790
your own work or your partner’s work?Larry
Karaszewski: Well, we’re very critical of

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:50.969
our own work because by the time it gets to
even first draft, that’s probably actually

00:04:50.969 --> 00:04:57.770
number ten for us. We’ve gone through it
so many times. So we generally prefer the

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.430
earlier drafts because they are kind of the
most pure, but we’ve been in this business

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.909
a long time so we do whatever it takes to
get it on the air. Film Courage: What book

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:10.050
can you recommend to some of the viewers on
screenwriting structure or adaption, anything?

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:17.479
Larry Karaszewski: I don’t really know?
Certainly in the old days I would read something

00:05:17.479 --> 00:05:24.409
like Syd Field, but I have no idea if that
would actually be something in today’s climate.

00:05:24.409 --> 00:05:29.810
I’d recommend a book that we wrote which
was about our screenplay for MAN ON THE MOON,

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:36.069
the Jim Carrey movie about Andy Kaufman. We
not only include the screenplay but we include

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:44.819
a very in-depth, how things change for the
screen and young screenwriters can really

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:49.050
look at that book and understand how a script
changes from when you write it until it makes

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:54.139
it on the screen. Film Courage: And lastly,
what film impacted you in terms of a true

00:05:54.139 --> 00:06:00.379
crime movie, like an Errol Morris film say
THE THIN BLUE LINE, different films in a similar

00:06:00.379 --> 00:06:06.169
genre to what you’ve done here? Larry Karaszewski:
I mean as a young man I saw the black and

00:06:06.169 --> 00:06:11.199
white version of IN COLD BLOOD, Richard Brooks’
version of that. And that definitely had a

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:17.339
gigantic effect on me, it terrified me. So
that was the first thing that came to mind.

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:24.319
Certainly David Fincher’s ZODIAC is an extraordinary
film, because it’s about not solving the

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:32.129
crime and it’s about how unsettling that
is. The fact that no matter how horrific true

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:38.369
crime stories tend to be, at the end there
is some kind of closure and ZODIAC is about

00:06:38.369 --> 00:06:42.840
not getting that closure. And what that does
to a person. How it tears you apart. So it’s

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.229
a very interesting film. Film Courage: Great!
Thank you very much. I appreciate your time.

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:45.309
Larry Karaszewski: Thank you!

